## Populate the Grafana dashboards

The dashboards need real HTTP traffic to display data. Run this from your project root:

```bash
#!/bin/bash
# Sends a mix of GET / POST / PUT / DELETE + errors + slow requests
# Covers: Request Rate, Error Rate, Latency p50/p95/p99, In-flight, Status breakdown

IDS=()
for round in $(seq 1 30); do
  # Create orders
  for item in "Widget-A" "Widget-B" "Gadget-X"; do
    r=$(curl -s -X POST http://localhost:{{ config.app_port }}/orders \
      -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
      -d "{\"item\":\"$item\",\"qty\":$((RANDOM % 5 + 1))}")
    id=$(echo "$r" | python3 -c "import sys,json; print(json.load(sys.stdin).get('id',''))" 2>/dev/null)
    [ -n "$id" ] && IDS+=("$id")
  done
  # Read
  curl -s http://localhost:{{ config.app_port }}/orders > /dev/null
  for id in "${IDS[@]:0:3}"; do curl -s http://localhost:{{ config.app_port }}/orders/$id > /dev/null; done
  # 4xx — unknown id
  curl -s http://localhost:{{ config.app_port }}/orders/not-found > /dev/null
  # 5xx — every 5 rounds (populates Error Rate panel)
  (( round % 5 == 0 )) && curl -s http://localhost:{{ config.app_port }}/orders/error > /dev/null
  # Slow endpoint — every 10 rounds (pushes p95/p99 latency up)
  (( round % 10 == 0 )) && curl -s http://localhost:{{ config.app_port }}/orders/slow > /dev/null &
  sleep 1
done
```

What each panel needs:

| Panel | What generates data |
|-------|-------------------|
| Request Rate | Any HTTP call to the app |
| Error Rate | 4xx (unknown IDs) and 5xx (`/orders/error`) |
| Latency p50/p95/p99 | Volume of requests — `/orders/slow` pushes the p99 up |
| In-flight Requests | Concurrent calls (the `&` on slow endpoint) |
| Responses by status | Mix of 200, 201, 404, 500 |

{% endif %}
## Key design decisions

- **`spring-boot-starter-opentelemetry`** — native SB4 starter, no Java agent.
- **`OtelLogbackInstaller`** — manually wires the Logback OTel Appender so `trace_id` appears in every log line sent to Loki.
- **`@Observed`** on service methods — Spring Boot 4 auto-creates a span + a Micrometer timer per annotated method.
- **OTel Collector as middleman** — app exports to Collector only; swap backends without touching app config.
- **OTLP port 4318 (HTTP)** by default. Override with `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT`.
- **Sampling** — `OTEL_SAMPLING_PROBABILITY=1.0` in dev. Set to `0.1` in staging, `0.05` in prod.
{% if config.use_database %}
- **Flyway** manages the DB schema. Hibernate is set to `ddl-auto: validate` only.
  Add your migrations to `src/main/resources/db/migration/`.
{% endif %}

## Observability & resilience patterns

### `@Observed` — automatic span + metric per method

Add `@Observed` on any Spring-managed bean method. Spring Boot 4 automatically creates:
- a **span** in Tempo (visible in the trace waterfall)
- a **Micrometer timer** (histogram) exported to Prometheus/Mimir — queryable as `p50/p95/p99` latency in Grafana

```java
import io.micrometer.observation.annotation.Observed;

@Service
public class OrderService {

    @Observed(name = "order.find", contextualName = "finding-order-by-id")
    public Order findById(Long id) { ... }
}
```

`name` → metric name (`order.find.seconds`). `contextualName` → span name in Tempo.

> **Requirement:** the class must be a Spring bean (`@Service`, `@Component`, `@Repository`) — `@Observed` on a plain object has no effect.

---

### `@Retryable` — automatic retry with backoff

Retries the method on the specified exception, up to `maxAttempts` times, with exponential backoff.
The `@Observed` span covers the **entire attempt sequence**, so you see the full latency including retries in Tempo.

```java
import org.springframework.retry.annotation.Backoff;
import org.springframework.retry.annotation.Retryable;
import org.springframework.retry.annotation.Recover;

@Observed(name = "payment.authorize", contextualName = "authorizing-payment")
@Retryable(
    retryFor  = PaymentGatewayException.class,
    maxAttempts = 3,
    backoff   = @Backoff(delay = 100, multiplier = 2, maxDelay = 500)
)
public PaymentResult authorize(Long orderId, BigDecimal amount) { ... }

// Called automatically after all retries are exhausted
@Recover
public PaymentResult authorizeRecover(PaymentGatewayException ex, Long orderId, BigDecimal amount) {
    return new PaymentResult(orderId, null, "FAILED");
}
```

> **Requirement:** `@EnableRetry` must be active. Add it once per application via a `@Configuration` class:
> ```java
> @Configuration
> @EnableRetry
> public class ResilienceConfig {}
> ```

---

### `@CircuitBreaker` — circuit breaker with fallback

After repeated failures within `openTimeout` ms, the circuit **opens** and all calls go directly to the `@Recover` fallback for `resetTimeout` ms — no further calls to the external service.

```java
import org.springframework.retry.annotation.CircuitBreaker;
import org.springframework.retry.annotation.Recover;

@Observed(name = "payment.settle", contextualName = "settling-payment")
@CircuitBreaker(
    include      = PaymentGatewayException.class,
    openTimeout  = 5_000L,   // circuit opens after failures within 5s
    resetTimeout = 20_000L   // circuit stays open for 20s, then half-opens
)
public PaymentResult settle(Long orderId) { ... }

@Recover
public PaymentResult settleRecover(PaymentGatewayException ex, Long orderId) {
    log.error("Circuit open — orderId={}", orderId);
    return new PaymentResult(orderId, null, "CIRCUIT_OPEN");
}
```

> **Note:** `@CircuitBreaker` is from `spring-retry`, not Resilience4j. It is simpler and sufficient for most use cases. For advanced features (half-open probing, per-instance state), switch to `resilience4j-spring-boot3`.

